WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.326
One Postgres server holds every payment.

2
00:00:05.326 --> 00:00:09.135
If it dies, payments stop until someone restores a backup.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.880
Every change goes first to the write-ahead log, the WAL.

4
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.209
A commit flushes the log to disk, and the data page is written later.

5
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:24.096
Replay that log onto a copy of the data, and you get a second, identical database: a standby.

6
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.320
pg_basebackup copies the data files over while the primary keeps running.

7
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:34.393
The standby then connects to the primary, and streams the log as it's written.

8
00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:37.632
It saves each record, and replays it onto its pages.

9
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:41.595
A replication slot remembers how far the standby has got.

10
00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:48.193
The primary keeps every record after that, even while the standby is down, until its own disk fills.

11
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.117
The standby catches up, but it stays a little behind.

12
00:00:52.117 --> 00:01:02.777
pg_stat_replication shows positions in the log, called LSNs: how far the primary has sent, how far the standby has flushed, and how far it has replayed.

13
00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:04.249
The gaps are the lag.

14
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:07.042
By default, replication is asynchronous.

15
00:01:07.042 --> 00:01:13.739
A commit waits only for the primary's disk, so the client hears OK while the record is still on its way.

16
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.723
Then the primary crashes.

17
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:18.288
Postgres won't fail over by itself.

18
00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:21.585
You, or a tool like Patroni, promote the standby.

19
00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:25.084
It replays what it has, and becomes the new primary.

20
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:31.892
The last two payments, 63 and 31 euros, were acknowledged, but never reached the standby.

21
00:01:31.892 --> 00:01:32.929
Both are gone.

22
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:40.314
Name the standby in synchronous_standby_names, and each commit also waits until the standby has flushed its record.

23
00:01:40.314 --> 00:01:43.685
Then every acknowledged payment is on the new primary.

24
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:50.456
The price is a round trip to the standby on every commit, and if the standby goes down, commits wait.

25
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:55.091
Asynchronous replication can lose the last few commits in a failover.

26
00:01:55.091 --> 00:01:58.040
Synchronous loses none that were acknowledged.
